ERROR REPORTS

Please send errors and suggestions to Frans Kaashoek and Robert Morris
(kaashoek,rtm@mit.edu).  The main purpose of xv6 is as a teaching
operating system for MIT's 6.1810, so we are more interested in
simplifications and clarifications than new features.

BUILDING AND RUNNING XV6

You will need a RISC-V "newlib" tool chain from
https://github.com/riscv/riscv-gnu-toolchain, and qemu compiled for
riscv64-softmmu.  Once they are installed, and in your shell
search path, you can run "make qemu".

UNDOBUDGET PROJECT NOTES

This course tree extends xv6 with shared process-group budgets. The original
xv6 acknowledgments above are preserved. For the implemented architecture,
approved cost policy, current enforcement limits, and reproducible tests, see
docs/design.md. For a fresh-boot presentation, follow docs/demo.md. The design
history and viva questions are in docs/journal.md and docs/viva.md.

From the repository root:

  make -j2 kernel/kernel fs.img
  make qemu CPUS=1

The default make target builds only the kernel. Tests that require one-time
group configuration or unused filenames should use a fresh, disposable image;
tests/regression.py and experiments/collect.py create scratch images without
replacing the repository's fs.img. Python agent tools are deterministic and
support only list_files, read_file, delete_file, and get_budget_status end to
end. Their write_file and execute_program requests are validated but are not
implemented by the guest executor. The approved cost table includes operations
that are not yet charged; see docs/design.md before claiming full coverage.
